#
# /etc/udev/rules.d/91-1bit-xhci-keepawake.rules - keep the Strix Halo xHCI controllers out of runtime suspend on
# the Bosgame BeyondMax (AXB35-02), where three of the four of them are otherwise dead ports (engine#823).
#
# Cycle 32, engine#823. Measured on the board (AXB35-02, BIOS 1.09, kernel 7.4.0-rc1-1bit) with nothing plugged into
# the rear ports or the Type-C ports:
#
#   controller   power state   PORTSC of every port
#   0000:c5:00.4  D3cold        0xffffffff     <- the function is off, the register block is unreadable
#   0000:c7:00.3  D3cold        0xffffffff
#   0000:c7:00.4  D3cold        0xffffffff
#   0000:c7:00.0  D0            0x2a0 RxDetect PP on the empty ports (this one carries the front USB-A, BT, JetKVM)
#
# and the SAME ports of 0000:c7:00.3, with only `power/control` forced to `on` at runtime, read
# `0x0a0002a0 Speed=0 Link=RxDetect PP WCE WOE` - port power on, SuperSpeed PHY up, wake-on-connect armed. So the
# ports are not electrically dead: the controller is switched off, and a controller that is off cannot raise a
# connect event, which is exactly the reported symptom (no kernel event at all, 0 interrupts since boot). The
# platform offers no way back either: the ACPI nodes (`XHC1` = \_SB_.PCI0.GPPA.XHC1, `XHC0/XHC3/XHC4` under
# \_SB_.PCI0.GPPC) declare power resources for D0/D3hot and no `_PR3`, and USB remote wake is disabled on every root
# hub, so nothing is armed to wake them.
#
# This rule is the same shape as 90-1bit-npu-power.rules one cycle earlier: instead of waiting for firmware, do not
# let the device enter the state the board cannot leave. The NPU rule is the precedent, including the `TEST` guard
# (inert on a kernel whose device has no `power/control`) and the narrow match so no other device is touched.
#
# Matched by BOARD, not by PCI id alone: the four Strix Halo xHCI functions exist on every Strix Halo machine, and on a
# machine whose firmware does wire port wake there is no reason to spend their idle power. The DMI match keeps this
# board-specific, which is what the measurement covers. The four, with the function numbers the PORTSC table above uses:
# 1022:1587 = c5:00.4, 1022:1588 = c7:00.0 (the awake one), 1022:1589 = c7:00.3, 1022:158b = c7:00.4. The two USB4 Host
# Routers (1022:158d = c7:00.5, 1022:158e = c7:00.6) are deliberately NOT in the alternation.
#
# `ACTION=="add|bind"`: on a normal boot xhci_hcd binds after udev is up; a module loaded before udev (initrd,
# coldplug) emits only `add`. If neither fires, `power/control` stays `auto` and the ports stay dark - the failure
# is silent (the box looks healthy, the ports are just absent), so the way to prove this rule ran is to read the four
# attributes back, not to trust the file's presence.
#
# One line on purpose: udev rules are parsed line by line and a trailing backslash is not a continuation here, so a
# wrapped rule silently matches nothing - which is how a fix like this looks like it shipped and does nothing.
ACTION=="add|bind", SUBSYSTEM=="pci", DRIVER=="xhci_hcd", ATTR{[dmi/id]board_name}=="AXB35-02", ATTR{vendor}=="0x1022", ATTR{device}=="0x1587|0x1588|0x1589|0x158b", TEST=="power/control", ATTR{power/control}="on"
